<!-- Example -->
<group name="local,syslog,sshd,">

  <!--
  Dec 10 01:02:02 host sshd[1234]: Failed none for root from 1.1.1.1 port 1066 ssh2
  -->
  <rule id="100001" level="5">
    <if_sid>5716</if_sid>
    <srcip>1.1.1.1</srcip>
    <description>sshd: authentication failed from IP 1.1.1.1.</description>
    <group>authentication_failed,pci_dss_10.2.4,pci_dss_10.2.5,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!--
  Rule 60110 ("User account changed", 4738) fires twice per interactive
  Windows unlock — see wazuh-integration/README.md for why. Two attempts at
  fixing this at the rule level did not work and are not repeated here:

  1. Bare <ignore>5</ignore>: relies on <ignore>'s default same_source_ip
     key. These alerts carry no srcip (a local Windows security event, not
     network-sourced), so it never found "the same source" — both copies
     still went out 225ms apart.
  2. <ignore>5</ignore> + <same_field>agent.id</same_field>: rejected at
     load time — analysisd requires <same_field> to be paired with
     <if_matched_sid>/frequency, it isn't valid as a plain <ignore>
     modifier ("Invalid use of frequency/context options. Missing
     if_matched on rule '60110'.").

  Lowering rule 60110's own level below the <level>7</level> forwarding
  threshold isn't right either — it's a real MITRE T1098 (Account
  Manipulation) detection when it fires for an actual attacker action, so
  blanket-suppressing it would blind us to genuine account tampering, not
  just this benign per-logon artifact.

  Deduping is instead done in custom-socore-integration.py itself (see
  DEDUP_WINDOW_S there), where the exact behavior is fully under our
  control and verifiable by reading the code, rather than depending on
  Wazuh rule-engine correlation semantics that twice didn't work as
  expected. Rule 60110 here is intentionally left at its base-ruleset
  definition (no override).
-->
